# The admin panel's credentials and the content store. Copy this file to
# .env.local and fill it in.
#
# .env* is already in .gitignore, so .env.local stays out of the repository —
# which is the whole point: the DEFAULTS these replace are checked in, visible
# in lib/admin/config.ts, and refused outright by a production build.
#
# !! EVERY VALUE BELOW IS BLANK ON PURPOSE AND MUST STAY BLANK. This file is
# !! committed. A real credential typed in here is a published credential, and
# !! it will not look like one — it will look like a helpful default. That has
# !! already happened once on this project: ADMIN_PASSWORD=Admin@123 was left
# !! in this file and pushed, which made it a password anyone with the repo
# !! could read. Fill in .env.local; leave this one empty.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SESSION_SECRET  (required in production)
#
# Signs the session cookie. Any change to it invalidates every signed-in
# session at once, which is also the only way to force a sign-out — there is no
# server-side session table. At least 32 characters; 64 is better and costs
# nothing.
#
#   openssl rand -base64 48
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(48).toString('base64'))"
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
SESSION_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ADMIN_EMAIL  (required in production)
#
# The one address that can sign in. Compared case-insensitively. Left filled in
# because it is not a secret and it is the address the client uses.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@hotcups.co.in

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE PASSWORD — set exactly ONE of the two below.
#
# ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH is the one to deploy. Generate it with:
#
#   npm run admin:hash "the password you want"
#
# and paste the whole pbkdf2$… string. The plaintext then exists nowhere on the
# server, so a leaked env dump does not hand anyone the password itself.
#
# !! ESCAPE THE DOLLARS IN A .env FILE. Next loads .env through dotenv-expand,
# !! which reads $NAME as a reference to another variable — and a PBKDF2 string
# !! is four fields separated by $. Pasted raw, "pbkdf2$210000$salt$hash" is
# !! loaded as "pbkdf2-salt-hash" and every sign-in fails with "that email and
# !! password do not match", which points at the password and says nothing
# !! about the file. Write it as pbkdf2\$210000\$salt\$hash here.
# !!
# !! A HOSTING DASHBOARD IS THE OPPOSITE: Vercel, Railway and the rest store
# !! what you type, so paste the UNESCAPED string there. `npm run admin:hash`
# !! prints both forms, each labelled.
#
# ADMIN_PASSWORD is the pragmatic alternative: the plaintext, which most hosts'
# environment-variable UIs make the easiest thing to paste. It is no weaker in
# transit — only in what a leaked dump reveals — and it is a perfectly
# reasonable choice for a site with one operator. Set one or the other, not
# both; the hash wins if both are present.
#
# !! AND IF YOU CHOOSE THE PLAINTEXT, CHOOSE A LONG ONE. There is no rate limit
# !! on /admin/login — app/admin/auth-actions.ts verifies and returns, with no
# !! attempt counter, no delay and no lockout — and the email is fixed and
# !! guessable. A 210,000-iteration hash made a short password survivable;
# !! plaintext means the password's own length is the entire defence.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH=
ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MONGODB_URI  (required on any host without a persistent filesystem)
#
# Where the panel's content lives. Set it and the store writes to MongoDB, one
# collection per section; leave it unset and the store falls back to
# data/content.json on disk.
#
# THE FILE BACKEND IS FOR LOCAL DEVELOPMENT ONLY, and the reason is not
# preference. Hostinger builds each release into a fresh checkout and deletes
# the old one, so a file written by the panel survives until the next push;
# Vercel's filesystem is read-only, so the write throws EROFS outright. Either
# way the client's edits do not persist. See lib/content/store.ts.
#
#   mongodb+srv://USER:PASSWORD@cluster.mongodb.net/?retryWrites=true&w=majority
#
# Escape any $ in the password, same rule as above.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
MONGODB_URI=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MONGODB_DB  (optional)
#
# Which database on that cluster. Unset, it resolves to `hotcups` in production
# and `hotcups_dev` everywhere else — so one cluster serves both and a laptop
# cannot overwrite the live site by forgetting which environment it is in. A
# database named in the URI's path wins over both.
#
# Moving existing data in: node scripts/content-to-mongo.mjs <database>
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
MONGODB_DB=
